Drag forces on three smooth circular cylinders were measured during towing through a water tank. The cylinders had diameters of 25.4, 38.1, and 50.8 mm, and each was tested at ten nominal speeds from 0.2 to 1.1 m s⁻¹. A calibrated load cell recorded force histories; mean force was calculated over a nominal steady interval and used to estimate the drag coefficient. Using the current analysis assumptions (water density 998 kg m⁻³, kinematic viscosity 1.004 × 10⁻⁶ m² s⁻¹, and reference length 0.60 m), the mean drag coefficients were 1.144, 1.153, and 1.178 for the 25.4, 38.1, and 50.8 mm cylinders, respectively. The corresponding Reynolds-number ranges were approximately 5.1 × 10³–2.8 × 10⁴, 7.6 × 10³–4.2 × 10⁴, and 1.0 × 10⁴–5.6 × 10⁴. The measurements show broadly similar coefficients across the tested diameters, but the present estimates are preliminary: water-temperature corrections, blockage effects, measurement uncertainty, and the treatment of a run with a suspected zero offset remain unresolved. These results provide an initial characterization of cylinder drag in the tow tank and identify the corrections required for a defensible comparison with published data.
